---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../config";

const title = `Portfolio — ${SITE_TITLE}`;
const description =
  "Design systems, developer tools, and content from Will Klein — examples of what I've built and how I work.";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header title={SITE_TITLE} />
    <main>
      <h1>Portfolio</h1>
      <p>
        People sometimes ask what I've worked on and how I work. This page is a
        quick answer: a few shipped things, the tools I've built, and the talks
        and writing where I've thought out loud about it. It leans toward design
        systems and developer experience. My work follows a pattern: figure out
        a problem, teach the solution, and build the process around it to make
        it stick.
      </p>

      <h2>Design systems, in practice</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          I helped maintain <a href="https://github.com/Workday/canvas-kit"
            >Canvas Kit</a
          >, Workday's open source design system. My focus wasn't just the
          components — it was adoption: onboarding new contributors, growing the
          internal developer community around the system, and making Canvas Kit
          the easy path so thousands of developers used it by choice rather than
          by mandate.
        </li>
        <li>
          I turned Canvas Kit's contribution story around. I started with
          stalled PRs that couldn't be merged. I rebuilt the process into one
          where contributors were supported, PRs were merged successfully, and
          new components were made by the community.
        </li>
        <li>
          I helped developers level up by pairing directly with them on their
          product work, teaching workshops, and holding weekly office hours
          where anyone could bring questions and get direct support.
        </li>
        <li>
          I've talked publicly about what makes that kind of adoption work, most
          recently in a talk called <em>"What makes a design system win"</em> at
          <a
            href="https://reactday.berlin/2024/#person-will-klein"
            target="_blank">React Day Berlin</a
          > and <a
            href="https://2024.weyweyweb.com/speakers/?speaker=Will%20Klein"
            target="_blank">Wey Wey Web</a
          > in 2024.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tools I've built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A <a
            href="https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/markdown-menu-for-github/jekgocfoijmbgcjejohdgmojaejofdpo?hl=en"
            target="_blank">browser extension</a
          > that adds a table of contents to GitHub READMEs and Markdown files. Years
          later, GitHub <a
            href="https://twitter.com/willklein_/status/1375688038651596810"
            target="_blank">shipped the same idea themselves</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://astsareawesome.com/" target="_blank"
            >ASTsareAwesome.com</a
          >, a little site I built to teach developers how to write their own
          dev tools — parsers, linters, codemods — through talks and workshops,
          including <em>"Start Building Your Own JavaScript Tools"</em> (<a
            href="https://gitnation.com/contents/start-building-your-own-javascript-tools"
            target="_blank">video</a
          >) and the <em>"Become a JavaScript Toolmaker"</em> workshop.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Content</h2>
      <p>
        I write and speak about design systems and developer tooling regularly —
        the full list is on my <a href="/speaking">speaking page</a>. A few that
        are most relevant here:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>"What makes a design system win"</em> — React Day Berlin & Wey Wey
          Web, 2024
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>"Testing our way to a better developer experience"</em> — React Day
          Berlin & Wey Wey Web, 2024
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>"Start Building Your Own JavaScript Tools"</em> — JSNation, 2023 (<a
            href="https://gitnation.com/contents/start-building-your-own-javascript-tools"
            target="_blank">video</a
          >)
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>"How Linters, Compilers & Other Cool Things Work"</em> — CascadiaJS,
          2018 (<a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JMqZgUNkqgk"
            target="_blank">video</a
          >)
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>"How to Win Developers and Influence Adoption"</em> — CascadiaJS, 2025
          (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fxGkzGZFxVo" target="_blank"
            >video</a
          >)
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Community Building</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond the work I've shipped, I've organized meetups and community
        events to bring developers together.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          I organized <a href="https://reactdenver.com/" target="_blank"
            >React Denver</a
          > for several years, co-ran <a
            href="https://denverscript.com/"
            target="_blank">DenverScript</a
          >, and founded HartfordJS — local developer communities built from
          scratch and run on a monthly cadence.
        </li>
        <li>
          I organized local workshops for <a
            href="https://twitter.com/gdcfpday"
            target="_blank">Global Diversity CFP Day</a
          > and mentor new speakers giving their first meetup or conference talk.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        If you want more context on any of this, or want to talk about a role,
        let's <a href="https://calendly.com/willklein">chat</a>.
      </p>

      <Footer />
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
